#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# atan

#% text
# `atan(x)` gives the principal inverse tangent of `x`, in `(-pi/2, pi/2)` for real `x`.

#% text
# `atan(x, y)` gives the angle of the point `(x, y)`, in `(-pi, pi]`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Exact values at 0, 1, `sqrt(3)`, `1/sqrt(3)` and their negatives; other exact arguments stay unevaluated.

#% item
# Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values; `atan(inf)` is `pi/2`. `atan` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
atan(1)
#% output
#= (1/4)*pi

#% cell
atan(sqrt(3))
#% output
#= (1/3)*pi

#% cell
atan(0.5)
#% output
#= 0.4636476090008061

#% cell
atan(inf)
#% output
#= (1/2)*pi

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The two-argument form picks the quadrant:

#% cell
atan(-1, -1)
#% output
#= (-3/4)*pi

#% cell
atan(-1, 1)
#% output
#= (3/4)*pi

#% text
# A complex argument:

#% cell
atan(1.0 + 1.0*im)
#% output
#= 1.0172219678978514 + 0.4023594781085251im

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Machin's formula for `pi`:

#% cell
n(4*(4*atan(1/5) - atan(1/239)), 30)
#% output
#= 3.141592653589793238462643383279503

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The derivative:

#% cell
d(atan(x), x)
#% output
#= (1 + x^2)^-1

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `tan`, `asin`, `acos`, `arg`
